WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.110 align:start position:0%
 
this morning on the dish  one woman's return to her roots somali  native hawa hassan grew up here in the  united states  but it was her homeland and reconnection  with her family  that sparked her passion a love of east  african cuisine  to bring the flavors of home here she  started a line of sauces  and just released a cookbook in bibi's  kitchen  i sat down with hassan to learn more  about the book and the journey that  brought her to it  papaya with vanilla sauce this is  chemo which is corn potatoes and green  peas  sauteed cabbage also from kenya sugo  from somalia  this table full of african dishes is  food entrepreneur hawa hassan my dream  was to always  be at the table and then slowly but  surely take over the table  and i mean i'm looking at this table  right now i'm like what is happening  you've taken  the recipes for these dishes are all in  hassan's new cookbook  in bibi's kitchen bibi means grandmother  in swahili  and it is bbs from eight african  countries who provided the recipes found  on hassan's pages  oh my god why did you want to do this  book  i wanted to do this book because i  hadn't seen people like myself reflected  in books in this way  and it's exploration and recipes through  conversation  with these women i really felt like they  were missing from the narrative  and in turn i was missing from the  narrative  hassan was born in somalia at the start  of the civil war in 1991  she fled with her mother and siblings to  a refugee camp in kenya  hassan was just four years old my  brother and i went from being children  to like young adult how do you do that  at four  i mean you just do what your mom says  and so everybody started pulling their  weight  her mother had hoped to move the family  to the u.s  but it was hassan alone who got the  chance to go to seattle  at age seven you had to be terrified no  i was playing my role in the family i  thought  here's an adventure i'll get a feel for  the land and then they'll come which i  didn't think it would take more than a  year for them to get documents to come  and as it turned out blackhawk happened  sponsorship for somali stopped my mother  remarried  and moved to norway where they still  live and you were still in seattle  i was still in seattle by middle school  hassan says  she was assimilating to the states i  joined the basketball team  i joined 4-h i became really what i call  an american teenager eating taco bell  was that right  i didn't know how to even do this you  know i  left all this behind did you miss home  you know i didn't give myself an  opportunity to do a lot of missing  because if i did i would have  cracked but what i later found out was  that i had missed the smell of home  or the hawaj that's in here that's what  i'd really miss  after high school hassan began earning a  living as a model  which exceeded expectations financially  but personally  left her wanting more i knew it was in a  space where i was welcomed you know i  was like oh we already have enough of  you  or she's not black enough she's not  white enough i started to really drown  in  narratives that were placed on me i was  everything  but hal hassan and i just kept  thinking about how do i get back to the  center of who i really am and that's  when i went on a quest to  reunite with my family after 15 years  in 2008 hassan finally visited her mom  and siblings in norway  it was incredible in every sense of the  word i had found a belonging  i had found things i'd longed for you  know in my culture we say paradise is at  the feet of your mom  and so to just be at home with her and  be in the kitchen with her to be on the  floor to wake up in the mornings  together  and the smell you got that smell i got  the ooms and the  houlage and all of that so once you had  sort of a taste of that some people  would think why not stay there  with them but you came back why because  i am incredibly independent  but hassan's mom did get her independent  daughter to think about  what was next how did you land on food  it just went back to how can i tell  stories and how can i change the  conversation about  who people are saying somalis are and  how can i do it through food  she saw space in the food industry with  a lack of african cuisine  this is the vanilla the vanilla sauce  for the lobster  oh my gosh it's a great holiday dish  in 2015 she launched her first venture  baspas the sauce line i just thought  hot sauce you know why not slowly inch  my way  onto the table by introducing people to  flavors of where i'm from  what started in a kitchen in harlem has  grown into nationwide distribution of  baspa sauces with a focus  on direct to consumer sales the advice  that was given to me was to write a  business plan  and so i knew two sauces from somalia  build it for a few years get a proposal  going for in bibi's kitchen  while doing that write a pitch for a  show  all of it has just come to pass adding  diversity hassan  says to an industry still in need of it  oh there's lots of tokenizing i won't be  in a space that doesn't have  people that look like me in it or is not  welcoming to folks like me if i get  inside i always ask for another person  who looks like me to come along and if  there's some pushback from that i know  how to pack up my bags and leave  hassan traces that steadfastness in  herself and her beliefs  to her mom traits also belonging  to the bbs in her cookbook all of them  are really proud  and a little grateful that these stories  are outside of themselves now  where we are as a society right now in  the conversations we're having  about race about inclusion about  changing the system does this fit in in  some way does happen absolutely  when you bring people together over a  plate of food a glass of wine  no conversation is too big or too small  i think that  for the first time in this country the  hard conversations are being had and so  my space in that is to be able to bring  people together put the food on the  table and allow my community to speak to  one another  and there's a saying that that she's  used before she wants to build longer  tables and shorter fences and that sort  of helps to have that conversation with  the food  she her plans are expanding that sauce  line right now and she's working on a  couple other projects but really an  incredible story to get to where she is  now  from where she came i love that she  missed the smell of home yeah right  doesn't that make sense it's all about  those smells  from childhood and we all have always  come back to you yes yeah i was looking  at the food on that table and it took me  back to kenyon tanzania we used to call  those little bites kidogos because they  kidogo and ki swahili means small so it  was just like  nibbling on food and getting a taste of  it all and her point was that that was  just missing from the industry because  it hadn't been brought there the same  with a book like this somebody hadn't  proposed it people didn't know  now it is out there right now it just  adds to the diversity and we'll go out  and buy it  can't wait to try them

